      <!-- INTRO -->
      <p><strong>Aerosol fill weight optimization</strong> is the process of setting and controlling how much product and propellant go into each can so that internal pressure, spray performance, and labeled net content all stay within their required ranges. It works by balancing the volume of liquid product against the headspace left for propellant vapor, since that headspace directly determines how much internal pressure the can will reach across its expected temperature range. This matters for aerosol manufacturers because a fill weight and headspace specification that looks correct on paper can still produce pressure failures, inconsistent spray performance, or regulatory net-content violations if it is not validated against real filling line tolerances and worst-case storage temperatures.</p>

      <!-- SECTION 1 -->
      <section id="section-1">
        <h2>What Fill Weight and Headspace Mean</h2>
        <p>Fill weight refers to the total weight of product concentrate and propellant dispensed into an aerosol can during manufacturing, while headspace is the remaining empty volume inside the sealed can above the liquid fill level, occupied by propellant vapor or compressed gas. These two figures are directly linked — for a can of fixed internal volume, increasing fill weight reduces headspace, and reducing fill weight increases it. Getting this relationship right is a core engineering task in aerosol production, not a simple dosing question, because headspace behaves according to gas physics that determine whether the finished can is safe, functional, and consistent from the first spray to the last.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Fill weight:</strong> Total mass of concentrate plus propellant added to each can</li>
          <li><strong>Headspace:</strong> Empty internal volume left for propellant vapor or compressed gas above the liquid fill</li>
          <li><strong>Can internal volume:</strong> Fixed by can size and construction, so fill weight and headspace are inversely related</li>
          <li><strong>Target range:</strong> Both are set with an upper and lower control limit, not a single exact number</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Because fill weight and headspace decisions are made early in the production process and are difficult to correct after crimping, they are typically finalized during formulation development and validated again during scale-up, a transition covered in detail in our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-manufacturing-101-guide" target="_top">Aerosol Manufacturing 101 guide</a>.</p>
      </section>

