      <!-- SECTION 2 -->
      <section id="section-2">
        <h2>Why Headspace Governs Internal Pressure</h2>
        <p>Internal can pressure is a function of headspace volume, propellant type, and temperature, and understanding this relationship is central to setting a safe fill specification. In a liquefied gas system such as an LPG or DME propellant, pressure remains relatively stable as long as some liquid propellant phase is present, because the liquid continuously replenishes the vapor phase as it is consumed — but reducing headspace still reduces the thermal expansion room available, which becomes critical at elevated temperature. In a compressed gas system using nitrogen or carbon dioxide, headspace volume has an even more direct effect, since compressed gas pressure follows general gas law behavior and changes continuously as headspace volume changes.</p>
        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Key Insight</strong>
          Headspace must be sized against the maximum credible storage or transport temperature the product will see, not typical ambient conditions — a can validated only at room temperature can still exceed safe pressure limits in a hot warehouse or delivery vehicle.
        </div>
        <p>This is why aerosol pressure testing protocols specify elevated test temperatures rather than relying on ambient measurements. For a deeper look at how these tests are structured, see our guide on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/vapor-pressure-measurement-aerosol-test-methods" target="_top">vapor pressure measurement test methods</a>, which explains the standard temperature points used to validate a can's pressure margin.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 3 -->
      <section id="section-3">
        <h2>Calculating a Headspace Target</h2>
        <p>Setting an initial headspace target starts with the propellant's vapor pressure data at the maximum expected storage temperature — available from sources such as the <a href="https://webbook.nist.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIST WebBook thermodynamic database</a> for common hydrocarbon and compressed gas propellants — cross-referenced against the aerosol can's rated proof and burst pressure from its manufacturer specification. Formulators build in a safety margin between the calculated maximum internal pressure at worst-case temperature and the can's rated pressure limits, since manufacturing variation, propellant blend tolerance, and filling equipment accuracy all introduce some variability around the target fill weight.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Propellant System</th>
                <th>Headspace Sensitivity</th>
                <th>Primary Design Consideration</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Liquefied gas (LPG, DME)</td><td>Moderate — pressure stable while liquid phase remains</td><td>Thermal expansion margin at elevated storage temperature</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Compressed gas (N₂, CO₂)</td><td>High — pressure follows headspace volume directly</td><td>End-of-life spray performance as gas depletes</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Bag-on-valve systems</td><td>Low direct sensitivity — propellant isolated from product</td><td>Propellant chamber volume sized independently of product fill</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Early-stage headspace calculations from vapor pressure data are a useful starting estimate, but they must be confirmed with actual filled samples tested across the intended temperature range before a specification is finalized for commercial production, since dimensional tolerances in real cans and valves introduce variation that a theoretical calculation cannot fully capture.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 4 -->
      <section id="section-4">
        <h2>Weight-Check Methods on the Filling Line</h2>
        <p>Verifying that each can meets its fill weight specification is typically handled through in-line automated checkweighing immediately after the gassing step, where scales weigh each can (or a statistically representative sample) and compare the result against upper and lower control limits derived from the target fill weight. Fill weight is closely tied to the aerosol fill test methodology described in <a href="https://www.astm.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM D3925</a>, a recognized standard covering the sampling and testing of aerosol products for fill weight and related quality attributes. Cans falling outside tolerance are automatically rejected or flagged for manual review, and control limit breaches are logged for quality trend analysis rather than treated as isolated events.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>100% in-line checkweighing:</strong> Every can weighed automatically as it exits the gassing station</li>
          <li><strong>Statistical sampling:</strong> A defined percentage of cans weighed periodically against control limits</li>
          <li><strong>Bench-scale calibration checks:</strong> Manual verification against calibrated reference weights to confirm in-line scale accuracy</li>
          <li><strong>Trend monitoring:</strong> Control charts tracking fill weight drift over a production run to catch gradual equipment drift before it produces out-of-tolerance cans</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="callout callout-amber">
          <strong>Rule of Thumb</strong>
          Checkweigher calibration drift is one of the most common causes of gradual, undetected fill weight deviation on a production line — scheduled calibration verification against traceable reference weights should be a fixed part of any filling line's standard operating procedure, not a reactive response to a quality complaint.
        </div>
        <p>For manufacturers evaluating whether to build in-house filling capability or work with a contract filler, weight-check infrastructure and calibration practices are a key differentiator worth assessing directly, a topic explored further in our guide to <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/how-contract-aerosol-filling-works" target="_top">how contract aerosol filling works</a>.</p>
      </section>

