      <!-- SECTION 5 -->
      <section id="section-5">
        <h2>Underfill vs Overfill: Consequences</h2>
        <p>Overfill and underfill carry meaningfully different risk profiles, and understanding both is necessary to set control limits that are neither dangerously loose nor unnecessarily tight. Overfilling reduces headspace below its design target, which raises the risk that internal pressure at elevated temperature will approach or exceed the can's rated burst pressure — a safety-relevant failure mode, not just a quality defect. Underfilling is generally not a safety risk, but it directly undermines product consistency: a can with less product than intended empties sooner than labeled, and toward the end of its life may show degraded spray characteristics as the ratio of remaining liquid to vapor phase shifts.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Overfill risk:</strong> Reduced headspace, elevated pressure risk at high temperature, potential valve or can failure</li>
          <li><strong>Underfill risk:</strong> Reduced usable product, inconsistent end-of-life spray performance, possible net-content labeling violation</li>
          <li><strong>Shared consequence:</strong> Both erode consumer trust in product consistency across a batch or between batches</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Net content accuracy is also a labeling compliance question in its own right, since declared weight or volume on the label must match what the product actually delivers within an accepted tolerance — a consideration that sits alongside the broader <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-can-safety-explained" target="_top">aerosol can safety framework</a> that governs pressure-related risk more broadly.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 6 -->
      <section id="section-6">
        <h2>Maintaining Consistency at Scale</h2>
        <p>Fill weight consistency becomes harder to maintain as production volume scales, since minor equipment wear, propellant blend variability, and ambient temperature shifts on the factory floor can all introduce small deviations that compound across a long production run. Manufacturers address this by combining tight equipment calibration schedules, statistical process control on fill weight data, and periodic full-batch pressure sampling rather than relying on any single control point in isolation.</p>
        <p>The crimping and gassing steps immediately surrounding the fill weight check are themselves sources of variability worth understanding in detail — valve seating quality and gas injection accuracy both interact with fill weight to determine final headspace, which is covered thoroughly in our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-crimping-gassing-leak-detection-explained" target="_top">crimping, gassing, and leak detection guide</a>. Teams should treat fill weight and headspace validation as an ongoing production control discipline rather than a one-time specification set during formulation development, since raw material, equipment, and environmental variables all shift over a product's commercial life. A broader view of how these technology and formulation decisions fit together across the aerosol development process is available in our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-technology-and-formulations" target="_top">Aerosol Technology &amp; Formulations</a> resource hub.</p>
      </section>

